Received: 8 August 2023 / Revised: 18 September 2023 / Accepted: 18 September 2023
## ABSTRACT
## KEYWORDS
MOFs, lead-free, α-particles, X-ray detection and imaging, dark current, fast response
## Introduction
Hybrid  organic–inorganic  detectors  are  an  emerging  class  of devices  for  direct  X-ray  detection  due  to  their  tunable functionalities  and  designable  architectures,  such  as  halide perovskites [1, 2] and mixed-matrix membranes [3, 4]. The hybrid perovskite  devices  have  good  photoelectric  performance  and carrier  transport  properties,  but  are  still  confronted  the  poor stability when exposed to moisture, oxygen, or high temperatures as a result of the hygroscopic and volatile nature of organic cations [5, 6]. In addition, doping with high atomic number $(Z)$ elements into  semiconductor  structures  and  compositing  high $Z$ nanoparticles  [4, 7, 8]  or  scintillators  [9]  into  organics  show excellent radiation detection performance. Despite the promising flexibility and high absorption of X-ray photons, they suffer from high dark currents of about $10^{4}\mathrm{pA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}^{-2}$ under an applied bias of $-10\mathrm{V}$ . This value is much higher than the dark current of state-ofthe-art  X-ray  detectors,  which  reaches  10 $\mathrm{{pA}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{mm}}^{-2}$ [10]. Furthermore, the increased impurities deteriorate carrier transport properties  (of  which  the  carrier  mobility  is  about $3.36~\times\$ $10^{-7}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ [4]. High leakage current and low carrier mobility limit the practical applications for X-ray imaging, dosimetry, realtime  dose  detection,  etc.  Therefore,  it  is  in  urgent  demand  to search  for  feasible,  low-cost,  and  chemically  stable  materials  for efficient  radiation  detectors  with  high  resistivity,  low  leakage current, high mobility, and radiation stability.
Metal–organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  composed  of  highly ordered  organic  linkers  and  inorganic  metal  cations,  have  been applied for catalysis, [11, 12] chemical sensing [13−15], and energy storage  and  conversion  [16, 17].  The  conductive  behavior  of MOFs  derived  from  effective  charge  transport  pathways constructed  by “through-bond”, “extended  conjugation”,  and “through-space” approaches,  has  been  studied  in  the  past  few years  [18].  With  the  advantages  of  superior  structures  and  high charge mobilities, MOFs have attracted extensive research interest in radiation detection [19−23]. The SCU-13 and SCU-14 MOFsbased  detectors  used  for  direct  X-ray  detection  were  reported, which observed significant X-ray-induced charge transport based on  semiconductive  MOF  powder  pellets,  indicating  their promising application for X-ray detection [19, 20]. However, those detectors  contain  heavy  metal  elements  such  as  Pb  and $\mathrm{~U~}$ to enhance $\mathrm{x}$ -ray  attenuation,  which  are  harmful  to  human  health and environment [19−21]. Lead-free MOFs used Cu [22] and Ni [23]  materials  have  been  synthesized  for  X-ray  detection  with extremely  slow  response  time,  which  severely  reduced  the detection  efficiency  and  limited  their  employment  in  medical diagnosis. Very recently, a single crystal block of SCU-15 MOFs showed potential application in X-ray detection [24], but the small size $(2.9\ \mathrm{\mm}\ \times\ 1.9\ \mathrm{\mm}\ \times\ 1.5\ \mathrm{\mm})$ of  the  crystal  limits  its interaction with the radiation. Therefore, developing a large-size MOFs-based detector combining the advantages of toxic-elementfree, low leakage current, fast response, and high charge transport is  a  new  chance  for  non-destructive  and  medical  diagnosis applications.
Zn-based  MOF  scintillators  have  been  observed  for  higher radiation  damage  resistance  than  the  corresponding  organic scintillators  under  protons, $\upgamma$ -rays,  electrons,  and  neutrons irradiation  [25].  Moreover,  the  introduced  zeolitic  imidazolate framework-8  (ZIF-8)  as  a  carrier  transport  layer  of  solar  cell structure, effectively improving transport efficiency and reducing recombination rate [26]. Due to the remarkable radiation stability of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based  MOFs  to  high-energy  radiation  and  the  excellent charge transport capability, it is anticipated to use Zn-based MOFs in  direct  X-ray  direction.  Here,  we  successfully  adopt  the  ZIF-8 pelleted wafer for radiation detection, which displays an extremely low leakage current $(1.27\mathrm{pA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}^{-2})$ , a high on/off ratio $(10^{3})$ , and a fast response time $\mathrm{'<100~ms)}$ for direct X-ray detection and a superior X-ray imaging capability with a spatial resolution of 1.2 $\mathrm{lp}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}^{-1}$ .  Moreover,  the  excellent  detection  efficiency  and  peak discrimination  observed  for  charged  particle  detection  in  MOFs (hydrogen-rich  molecules)  are  promising  for  their  further application in thermal and high-energy neutron detection.
## 2    Experimental section
## 2.1    The synthesis of ZIF-8 MOF
2-Methylimidazole $(0.821\mathrm{g},10\mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ (0.744 $\mathrm{\underline{{g}}},\ 2.5\ \mathrm{\mmol}_{.}$ )  were  separately  dissolved  in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ solution  of methanol. The two solutions were quickly mixed and left at room temperature  for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .  The  precipitates  were  collected  by centrifugation, then washed with methanol (3 times), and finally soaked in methanol.
## 2.2    Fabrication of ZIF-8-based X-ray detector
ZIF-8 wafer was obtained by pelleting at 5 Mpa for 5 min. Planar ZIF-8  detectors  were  fabricated  by  depositing  Au  electrodes $\mathrm{~\'~}\sim80\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness)  via  vacuum  evaporation  on  two  opposite sides  of  ZIF-8  wafer.  Then, $\mathrm{Au/ZIF{-}8/A u}$ heterostructure  was bonded to the sample holder using carbon paste attached to one of the electrodes and Cu wire to the other.
## 2.3    Basic characterization
Powder  X-ray  diffraction  (XRD)  was  taken  using  Bruker  D8 Advance diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha1$ irradiation (wavelength $\lambda=$ $1.5406\textup{\AA})$ ) operated at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40~\mathrm{mA}$ . The scanning angle was set  in  the  range  of $5^{\circ}-40^{\circ}$ ,  and  the  scanning  rate  selected  was $2.5^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ .  The  morphology  of  ZIF-8  wafer  was  characterized using a scanning electron microscope (ZEISS Sigma 300). Optical transmission  spectra  were  recorded  at  room  temperature  using Shimadzu UV-3150 spectrometer. Current–voltage (I–V) characteristics were measured with Keithley Model 6517B.
## 2.4    Radiation detection measurements
The  time-of-flight  (TOF)  technique  was  used  to  evaluate  the carrier mobility of ZIF-8-wafer. Pulsed height spectra as a function of bias voltage were obtained from samples irradiated from an uncollimated $^{241}{\mathrm{Am}}\textcircled{\omega}5.49$ MeV  alpha  particle  source  at  room temperature in air. The detector was powered by a high-voltage power  supply  (ORTEC  556).  Pulse  shape  information  was simultaneously  recorded  by  connecting  the  output  of  the preamplifier  to  a  high-speed  waveform  digitizer  card.  ZIF-8 detectors were connected to a charge-sensitive preamplifier (eV550) and the signals were shaped by the amplifier (ORTEC 570), with a charge sensitivity of $3.6~\mathrm{mV\cdotfC^{-1}}$ . The digital pulse shaper was used to measure the pulse amplitude and the rise time of the preamplifier from $10\%$ to $90\%$ to fit the mobility. The final signal was evaluated by a multichannel analyzer (ORTEC EASY-MCA8K).
An Amptek Mini-X X-ray tube was used as $\mathrm{X}\mathrm{\Omega}$ -ray source with a tube voltage of $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV_{p}}}$ and a tube current from 10 to $90\upmu\mathrm{A}.$ The device  was  placed  in  a  dark-shielded  Pb  box  to  prevent disturbance from ambient light at room temperature. The induced photocurrents  were  recorded  by  Keithley  6517B  instrument  at various bias voltages. The X-ray imaging capability of the ZIF-8 device was measured by moving the objects on a home-built $\mathbf{X-Z}$ scanning system.
## 3    Results and discussion
## 3.1    Wafer quality and electrical characterization
## 3.2    Application  properties  for $\pmb{\alpha}$ -particles  radiation detection
Low  leakage  current  and  high  mobility  are  critical  to  achieve detection  of  full-energy  peaks.  The  working  schematic  of Au/ZIF-8/Au device for detecting α-particles $(5.49\mathrm{MeV}^{241}\mathrm{Am})$ is shown in Fig. 2(a). The conventional TOF technique was used to characterize the transport behavior of ZIF-8 detectors. The pulse fall  time $(\tau)$ for  the  hole  signal  (normally, $10\%{-}90\%$ amplitude time  was  selected)  has  been  measured  (Fig. 2(b)),  and  the histogram  of  pulse  fall  time  distribution  under $150\mathrm{~V~}$ bias  is shown  in Fig. 2(c).  The  results  under  other  biases  are  shown  in Fig. S4 in the ESM. These values decrease as the applied voltage increases, indicating higher voltage corresponds to higher current. As shown in Fig. 2(d), the mobility $(\mu)$ could be assessed by the linear fitting of Eq. (1)
$$
\mu=\frac{\nu}{E}=\frac{d^{2}}{V\times\tau}
$$
where $\nu$ is  for  carrier  drift  velocity, $E$ is  electric  field, $V$ is  bias voltage, and $d$ is carrier drift distance, that is equal to the distance between  two  electrodes.  Accordingly,  the  corresponding  hole mobilities  of  the  ZIF-8  device  are  calculated  to  be $0.2\ \pm\ 0.01$ $\mathsf{c m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ by  linearly  fitting  the  electric  field-dependent  drifting velocity  (Fig. 2(d)).  This  value  is  comparable  to  the  mobility  of organic  semiconductors  but  lower  than  that  of  inorganic semiconductors.  It  is  possible  to  improve  the  transportation efficiency  of  MOFs  by  implementing  design  strategies,  such  as increasing the host–guest interactions, shortening the transmission  pathway,  and  enhancing $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking  interaction between organic moieties. More importantly, the full-energy peaks of ZIF-8 device could be observed under the negative voltage at room  temperature,  of  which  the  pulses  were  collected  from  the preamplifier. A superior energy resolution of $43.78\%$ (full width at half maximum (FWHM)) is achieved at $-300\mathrm{~V~}$ bias (Fig. 2(e)). As  the  bias  voltage  increases,  the  position  of  the  peak  centroid shifts  higher  up  the  channel,  demonstrating  effective  charge collection. In addition, ZIF-8 detectors present the potential to be used as fast neutron solid-state detectors due to the high number of  proton  recoil  events  within  hydrogen-rich  organic  molecules. The  corresponding  density  of  hydrogen  atoms  is  calculated  by Eq. (2)
$$
n\left({\mathrm{molecules}}\right)={\frac{\rho}{M}}\times N
$$
where $\rho$ is density $(\rho_{\mathrm{ZIF-8}}=0.95~\mathrm{g{\cdot}c m^{-3})}$ , $M$ is relative molecular mass $(\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , and $N$ is Avogadro constant of $6.02\times10^{23}\mathrm{n{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ . The number of ZIF-8 molecules in $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ detector is calculated to be $2.99\times10^{22}\mathrm{n}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , which is comparable to the hydrogen density $(4.22\ \times\ 10^{22}\ \mathrm{\n{\cdot}c m^{-3}})$ in  organic  fast  neutron  detectors  and determines  the  neutron-detector  interaction  possibility.  The  first evaluation  of $\mathtt{a}$ -particle  detection  capability  of  MOFs-based detector with good charge collection and peak identification has been achieved, which opens the way for the application of MOFs in charge counting spectroscopy of radiation sources.
## 3.3    X-ray detection and imaging performance
$$
S=\frac{\Delta J}{D}
$$
$$
\Delta J=\frac{I_{\mathrm{ON}}-I_{\mathrm{OFF}}}{A}
$$
Owing to low dark current, high on/off ratio, and highly stable photocurrent, ZIF-8 detector exhibits a very low level of baseline drift and a highly stable photocurrent output signal, resulting in excellent  direct  imaging  capabilities.  Unlike  other  MOFs-based scintillators (operated based on the photoluminescence effect), ZIF8 based semiconductor detector can directly convert high-energy rays into electrical signals [36]. As shown in Figs. $3(\mathrm{g)}$ and $3(\mathrm{h})$ , Xray image of a needle is recorded at electric field of $100\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . It clearly shows the similarity in shape and size between the object and the corresponding X-ray image, demonstrating that the object is  clearly  resolved  without  deviation.  Furthermore,  the  line-pair card  was  used  in  the  imaging  system  under  the  same  test conditions.  A  spatial  resolution  of $1.2\ \mathrm{lp}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}^{-1}$ is  achieved  by checking the abruptness of contrast changes at the boundary (Fig. 3(i)).  Also,  ZIF-8  detector  exhibits  good  stability  in  long-term operation, which is critical for practical imaging applications (Fig. S7 in the ESM).
To  further  verify  the  capability  of  ZIF-8  detector  for  medical imaging  applications,  the  short  pulse  X-ray  response  was investigated (Fig. 4). For dynamic imaging, the frame rate is a key parameter  that  determines  the  fluency  of  X-ray  image.  The transient $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray  response  of  ZIF-8  detector  was  irradiated  by pulsed X-rays produced by a chopper wrapped with $1\mathrm{mm}$ thick lead sheet. A typical $J{-}t$ curve for ZIF-8 detector is shown in Fig. 4(a),  irradiated  by  pulsed  X-rays  at  a  frequency  of $2~\mathrm{{Hz}}$ at  an electric field of $291.67\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}^{-1}$ . The rise time (from $10\%$ to $90\%$ of the  saturated  X-ray  signal)  and  fall  time  are  88  and $107\ \mathrm{ms},$ respectively,  which  roughly  meet  the  common  requirements  for dynamic  digital  radiography.  The  frame  rate  used  for  dynamic medical imaging is 10–20 frames per second, which is equivalent to a rise time of $50\mathrm{-}100~\mathrm{ms}$ . Figure $4(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}})$ shows 100-cycle response of ZIF-8 detector, which shows the good stability of ZIF-8 device. Also, the same transient response test results at $3\:\mathrm{Hz}$ are shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM, which keeps rising photocurrent. Therefore, the fast response time can effectively improve detection efficiency, which is beneficial to medical imaging and security screening.
## 4    Conclusions
In  this  work,  we  successfully  adopt  ZIF-8  pelleted  wafer  for radiation detection with low dark current and fast response. ZIF-8 based detector can effectively convert X-ray photons to electrical current signals under continuous X-ray radiation, which presents a promising X-ray sensitivity with the value of $70.82~\upmu\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Gy}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a low detection limit of $3.475~\upmu\mathrm{Gy}_{\mathrm{air}}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ under $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kV_{p}}}$ X-ray exposure. The low dark current $(1.27\mathrm{pA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mm}^{-2})$ ), high on/off ratio $(10^{3})$ ,  and  fast  response  time $(<100~\mathrm{ms})$ )  in  a  very  low  level  of baseline  drift  and  a  highly  stable  photocurrent  output  signal, exhibit  a  superior $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray  imaging  capability  with  a  spatial resolution of $1.2\mathrm{lp}\mathrm{\cdotmm}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the high resistivity $(2.88\times$ $10^{12}~\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm})$ and high mobility $(0.2\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ contribute to good peak discrimination with energy resolution of $\sim43.78\%$ , when the detector  is  illuminated  by  uncollimated $^{241}\mathrm{Am}@5.48$ MeV  α- particles.  The  excellent  detection  efficiency  and  charged-particle spectroscopy  in  MOFs  (hydrogen-rich  molecules)  indicated  the potential applications of MOFs-based electronic devices in highenergy ray and fast neutron detection.
The above results demonstrate the fast response, high stability, and  charged  particle  detection  capability  displayed  by  MOF materials  when  employed  as  radiation  detectors.  Despite  the promising results, MOF devices are significantly limited in their optoelectronic  performance  and  transport  characteristics  due  to factors such as grain boundaries when compared to conventional inorganic detectors, perovskites, etc (Table S2 in the ESM). Given the  intrinsic  advantages  of  MOFs,  including  designable architectures and chemical compositions as well as single-crystal growth  techniques,  more  versatile  candidates  with  much improved  capabilities  in  this  system  could  be  seen  in  the  near future.
